1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,465
A map says three blocks east.

2
00:00:02,465 --> 00:00:04,578
A friend says four steps north.

3
00:00:04,578 --> 00:00:06,642
A drone climbs five metres up.

4
00:00:07,042 --> 00:00:09,052
Every instruction has two parts.

5
00:00:09,052 --> 00:00:10,781
How much, and which way.

6
00:00:11,231 --> 00:00:15,138
At college, Aarav gives a new
student directions to the library.

7
00:00:15,488 --> 00:00:18,132
Three steps east, then four steps north.

8
00:00:18,132 --> 00:00:20,394
But what exactly is one step east?

9
00:00:20,894 --> 00:00:22,748
That tiny arrow has a name.

10
00:00:22,748 --> 00:00:25,378
It is the building block of every vector.

11
00:00:26,028 --> 00:00:27,773
So what is a unit vector?

12
00:00:28,123 --> 00:00:31,778
A unit vector is a vector
with a size of exactly one.

13
00:00:31,778 --> 00:00:33,646
It only shows a direction.

14
00:00:34,046 --> 00:00:36,288
It has no unit and no dimension.

15
00:00:36,288 --> 00:00:37,869
It is a pure pointer.

16
00:00:38,269 --> 00:00:40,606
We write it with a small hat on top.

17
00:00:40,606 --> 00:00:41,824
Say it as A hat.

18
00:00:42,224 --> 00:00:45,273
Three special unit vectors
point along the axes.

19
00:00:45,623 --> 00:00:46,851
i hat along x.

20
00:00:46,851 --> 00:00:48,080
j hat along y.

21
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:51,002
And k hat along z, for three dimensions.

22
00:00:51,652 --> 00:00:55,343
Let's build a vector out of unit
steps in our motion notebook.

23
00:00:55,694 --> 00:00:57,594
Take three steps of i hat.

24
00:00:57,594 --> 00:00:59,593
That is three units along x.

25
00:00:59,993 --> 00:01:01,871
Now four steps of j hat.

26
00:01:01,871 --> 00:01:03,904
That is four units along y.

27
00:01:04,304 --> 00:01:05,668
Join start to end.

28
00:01:05,668 --> 00:01:08,562
The vector is three i hat plus four j hat.

29
00:01:08,962 --> 00:01:12,161
Now let's stretch this arrow
and watch its unit vector.

30
00:01:12,561 --> 00:01:14,826
The arrow grows from five to ten.

31
00:01:14,826 --> 00:01:17,091
But its unit vector stays at one.

32
00:01:17,091 --> 00:01:18,449
Look at the graph.

33
00:01:18,449 --> 00:01:19,650
One line climbs.

34
00:01:19,650 --> 00:01:21,153
The other stays flat.

35
00:01:21,653 --> 00:01:23,469
Slow it down and turn the arrow.

36
00:01:23,819 --> 00:01:28,302
The unit vector turns with it, always
with its tip on a circle of radius

37
00:01:28,302 --> 00:01:29,202
one.

38
00:01:28,794 --> 00:01:30,568
Only the direction changes.

39
00:01:31,218 --> 00:01:33,566
Now, how do we find a unit vector?

40
00:01:33,566 --> 00:01:34,959
One step at a time.

41
00:01:35,309 --> 00:01:36,215
Step one.

42
00:01:36,215 --> 00:01:38,835
Divide the vector by its own size.

43
00:01:38,835 --> 00:01:41,577
A hat equals vector A divided by A.

44
00:01:42,327 --> 00:01:43,227
Step two.

45
00:01:43,185 --> 00:01:44,419
Check its size.

46
00:01:44,419 --> 00:01:46,207
A divided by A is one.

47
00:01:46,207 --> 00:01:48,696
So A hat really is a unit vector.

48
00:01:49,446 --> 00:01:50,435
Step three.

49
00:01:50,435 --> 00:01:52,089
The units cancel too.

50
00:01:52,089 --> 00:01:54,840
Metres divided by metres leave nothing.

51
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:57,117
So a unit vector has no unit.

52
00:01:57,867 --> 00:01:58,820
Step four.

53
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:00,112
Turn it around.

54
00:02:00,112 --> 00:02:03,659
Any vector equals its size
times its unit vector.

55
00:02:03,659 --> 00:02:05,586
A equals A times A hat.

56
00:02:06,336 --> 00:02:10,224
In components, A equals
A x i hat plus A y j hat.

57
00:02:10,224 --> 00:02:13,058
Size times direction, along each axis.

58
00:02:13,808 --> 00:02:14,835
Remember this.

59
00:02:14,835 --> 00:02:19,635
A vector is a size times a direction,
and the direction is the unit vector.

60
00:02:20,335 --> 00:02:22,791
Let's compare two arrows side by side.

61
00:02:23,141 --> 00:02:25,526
The vector three i plus four j.

62
00:02:25,526 --> 00:02:26,965
Its size is five.

63
00:02:27,365 --> 00:02:31,694
Its unit vector is zero point
six i plus zero point eight j.

64
00:02:31,694 --> 00:02:34,454
Same direction, but its size is one.

65
00:02:35,054 --> 00:02:36,146
Now you try.

66
00:02:36,146 --> 00:02:38,288
Is two i hat a unit vector?

67
00:02:38,288 --> 00:02:39,645
Pause and decide.

68
00:02:42,845 --> 00:02:43,745
No!

69
00:02:43,319 --> 00:02:46,729
Two i hat points along
x, but its size is two.

70
00:02:46,729 --> 00:02:49,975
A unit vector must have
a size of exactly one.

71
00:02:50,625 --> 00:02:51,525
One more.

72
00:02:51,481 --> 00:02:54,082
Is i hat plus j hat a unit vector?

73
00:02:54,082 --> 00:02:55,366
Pause and think.

74
00:02:58,566 --> 00:02:59,466
No!

75
00:02:59,035 --> 00:03:03,291
Its size is the square root of
two, about one point four one.

76
00:03:03,291 --> 00:03:06,397
Divide by root two to
make it a unit vector.

77
00:03:07,047 --> 00:03:08,783
Now, one worked example.

78
00:03:09,133 --> 00:03:12,421
Aarav walks three metres
east and four metres north.

79
00:03:12,421 --> 00:03:15,059
Find the unit vector of his displacement.

80
00:03:15,559 --> 00:03:19,528
His displacement is three i
hat plus four j hat, in metres.

81
00:03:20,278 --> 00:03:24,274
Its size is the square root of
three squared plus four squared.

82
00:03:24,274 --> 00:03:25,719
That is five metres.

83
00:03:26,469 --> 00:03:27,751
Divide by five.

84
00:03:27,751 --> 00:03:32,691
The unit vector is zero point six
i hat plus zero point eight j hat.

85
00:03:33,441 --> 00:03:37,994
Check: zero point six squared plus
zero point eight squared is one.

86
00:03:37,994 --> 00:03:39,539
So its size is one.

87
00:03:40,189 --> 00:03:41,655
Now, the exam trap.

88
00:03:42,005 --> 00:03:46,756
Many students write the unit vector
with a unit, like zero point six i

89
00:03:46,756 --> 00:03:49,041
plus zero point eight j metres.

90
00:03:49,391 --> 00:03:52,726
But the metres cancel when
you divide by the size.

91
00:03:52,726 --> 00:03:54,627
A unit vector has no unit.

92
00:03:54,627 --> 00:03:56,678
The unit stays with the size.

93
00:03:57,328 --> 00:03:58,882
Where is this asked?

94
00:03:58,882 --> 00:04:03,826
Mostly inside bigger questions: find
a vector of a given size along a given

95
00:04:03,826 --> 00:04:04,726
direction.

96
00:04:05,270 --> 00:04:06,663
Exam level now.

97
00:04:06,663 --> 00:04:09,895
Vector P is six i plus eight j newtons.

98
00:04:09,895 --> 00:04:13,671
Vector Q has a size of
fifteen newtons along P.

99
00:04:13,671 --> 00:04:14,768
What is Q?

100
00:04:14,768 --> 00:04:16,219
Pause and solve.

101
00:04:21,619 --> 00:04:23,088
The answer is B.

102
00:04:23,088 --> 00:04:25,434
Nine i plus twelve j newtons.

103
00:04:25,934 --> 00:04:30,362
The size of P is the square root
of thirty six plus sixty four.

104
00:04:30,362 --> 00:04:31,437
That is ten.

105
00:04:31,937 --> 00:04:36,459
So P hat is zero point six
i plus zero point eight j.

106
00:04:36,459 --> 00:04:38,216
Multiply by fifteen.

107
00:04:38,216 --> 00:04:40,636
Q is nine i plus twelve j.

108
00:04:41,236 --> 00:04:42,700
Here is your recap.

109
00:04:42,700 --> 00:04:44,205
Screenshot this card.

110
00:04:47,405 --> 00:04:50,242
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:04:50,642 --> 00:04:51,542
One.

112
00:04:51,165 --> 00:04:53,761
Why does a unit vector have no unit?

113
00:04:54,661 --> 00:04:55,561
Two.

114
00:04:55,132 --> 00:04:58,043
How do you find the unit
vector of any vector?

115
00:04:58,943 --> 00:04:59,843
Three.

116
00:04:59,586 --> 00:05:02,914
Is i hat plus j hat a
unit vector, and why?

117
00:05:04,014 --> 00:05:06,948
Next time: a point on
a map has a position.

118
00:05:06,948 --> 00:05:10,518
When it moves, how do we
write the change as a vector?

119
00:05:10,518 --> 00:05:12,591
Position and displacement vectors.
